from pathlib import Path
R=Path(__file__).resolve().parents[2]
p=R/'main.tex';s=p.read_text()
for n in ['04','09','13','14']:
 s=s.replace(f'artifacts/editorial_accent_20261022/pdf/Figure{n}_Accent.pdf',f'artifacts/semantic_revision_20261022/pdf/Figure{n}_Semantic.pdf')
def caption(label,new):
 global s
 end=s.index('\\label{'+label+'}');start=s.rfind('\\caption{',0,end)
 s=s[:start]+'\\caption{'+new+'}\n'+s[end:]
caption('fig:task-function-grid',r'From task requirements to Harness modules. (a) Columns identify task families and rows identify shared temporal functions. Read a column to assemble requirements for one task, or a row to compare how a function serves different tasks. Shaded regions and solid boundaries mark a forecast horizon, a diagnostic interval, information beyond the forecast origin $t_0$, and an admissible action range. (b) A demand-planning workflow combines general execution, shared temporal preparation, forecasting, and inventory decisions. The forecast panel separates observed history from a schematic prediction and its range. The grid synthesizes requirements illustrated by TimeSeriesScientist \citep{timeseriesscientist2025}, SAGE-AD \citep{sagead2026}, MERIT \citep{merit2025}, and FinMem \citep{finmem2023}.')
caption('fig:merit-augmentation',r'MERIT\textquotesingle s construction of positive samples for representation learning \citep{merit2025}. Similarity retrieval and LLM relevance review produce a reference set $R$, which informs transformation selection. The schematic candidates contrast small jitter that preserves peak order with a permutation that changes it; these illustrate possible review outcomes. The review agent compares each view with the target sequence and returns a judgment and rationale. Accepted views enter memory $M$, while rejection restarts retrieval. The lower band shows the downstream role of these choices: accepted views and retrieved neighbors form positive samples for contrastive learning with a shared encoder. Adapted from the original retrieval, augmentation, review, and learning mechanisms.')
caption('fig:benchmark-qa',r'An evidence-conditioned answer in TS-Agent \citep{tsagent2025}. The anomaly-location question and its required predicates follow the original method; the sequence and middle-region outcome are illustrative. A numerical tool analyzes the native series and records an observation. The evidence gate checks both anomaly presence and location before accepting the answer; an uncovered requirement triggers another analytical action. The lower chain identifies the records needed to trace the answer to the series, tool invocation, and structured observation. This example shows how temporal question answering can evaluate evidence acquisition and answer verification within a larger Agent workflow.')
caption('fig:benchmark-infrastructure',r'TIMECLAW\textquotesingle s runtime and capability evolution \citep{timeclaw2026harness}. The generalist LLM selects an operation and its parameters; temporal tools execute on a full-precision numerical workspace and return compact observations. Episodic memory retrieves successful analytical traces using signal and contextual keys, together with a transferable rationale. The lower path converts recurring executed procedures into candidate tools, evaluates them on held-out similar tasks, and registers accepted tools for later episodes. The two paths identify the workspace, memory, and tool-set state needed to reproduce the system. Adapted from the original workspace, tool, memory, and evolution mechanisms.')
s=s.replace('Figure~\\ref{fig:merit-augmentation} shows this separation between retrieval, transformation, and review.','Figure~\\ref{fig:merit-augmentation} follows these decisions through a schematic augmentation example and shows how the resulting positive set enters the representation learner.')
s=s.replace('Figure~\\ref{fig:benchmark-qa} follows the question-to-tool-to-answer sequence. Inspecting the trace reveals which observation supports the response and whether an additional tool call would resolve an ambiguity.','Figure~\\ref{fig:benchmark-qa} instantiates the method\'s anomaly-location question: the evidence must establish both that an anomaly exists and which part of the series contains it. The numerical observation fills these requirements in the evidence log; an uncovered requirement directs the next action. Inspecting this trace reveals how the answer follows from the available observations.')
start=s.index('\\section{Conclusion}')+len('\\section{Conclusion}')
end=s.index('% Keep the references',start)
s=s[:start]+'''\n\nThis survey explains how to build Time-Series Agents from general-purpose capabilities through a module-level taxonomy of General Harness, General Time-Series Harness, and Task-Specific Harness. These scopes distinguish reusable execution mechanisms, shared temporal functionality, and procedures tied to a task objective, while the LLM connects requests, tool actions, and observations. Comparing methods through their mechanisms, assumptions, and feedback clarifies which components a new scenario can reuse and which require adaptation. Linking these choices to development resources and evaluation protocols provides a construction path from scenario requirements to tested systems, spanning forecasting, synthesis, anomaly diagnosis, and decision support. The same framework motivates research on transferable temporal procedures, experience under changing regimes, and evaluation of downstream actions. The accompanying structured literature records support continued comparison as new modules and tasks emerge.\n'''+s[end:]
p.write_text(s)
print('Updated four figures, captions, reading guidance, and one-paragraph conclusion')
